Comparative Reflection
All three plans have problem solving, engineering, teamwork, and continued learning in common. Even though the specific careers are different, they all involve using technical knowledge to solve real problems and contribute to meaningful projects. This tells me that my core direction is not limited to one exact job. I want a career where I can use both technical skills and my ability to work well with other people.
Plan A feels the most authentic to who I am right now because nuclear engineering is the direction I have chosen and the degree I currently want to pursue. I am interested in the energy field and want to see where nuclear engineering can take me. Plan C challenges me the most because becoming an engineering project manager would require me to eventually take on more responsibility for other people, deadlines, and major decisions instead of focusing mainly on my own technical work.
If I had to commit to Plan B tomorrow, my first step would be to meet with an academic advisor and determine how my current courses would transfer into mechanical engineering. Second, I would review the mechanical engineering degree requirements and adjust my future course schedule. Third, I would research mechanical engineering internships in the energy and manufacturing industries so I could begin gaining experience in the new field as soon as possible.




